    <node id="范蠡">
      <data key="d0">范蠡</data>
      <data key="d1">人物</data>
      <data key="d2">Fan Li (范蠡) was a distinguished statesman, strategist, and advisor of the Yue Kingdom during the Spring and Autumn Period of ancient China. Originally hailing from Wan in the Chu Kingdom, he was renowned for his exceptional intelligence, calm demeanor, and strategic acumen. Fan Li served as a trusted advisor to King Goujian of Yue and played a pivotal role in the kingdom's victory over its rival, the Wu Kingdom. Alongside his close ally Wen Zhong, Fan Li devised critical strategies, including the "Nine Strategies to Defeat Wu," which were instrumental in Yue's triumph. His contributions earned him recognition as a hero who helped restore Yue's strength and sovereignty.

Fan Li's resourcefulness and ingenuity were widely acknowledged, and he was sometimes referred to as "Fan the Madman" during his time in Chu. He was also skilled in swordsmanship and sought to recruit the talented swordswoman A Qing, recognizing her potential to aid Yue's cause. Fan Li maintained a supportive relationship with A Qing, valuing her contributions to the kingdom's efforts.

In addition to his political and military achievements, Fan Li's personal life has drawn significant historical and literary interest. He is famously associated with Xi Shi, one of the Four Beauties of ancient China, with whom he shared a romantic relationship. Their emotional connection has been the subject of various accounts, highlighting the interplay between his personal and political commitments. Despite his ties to Xi Shi, Fan Li remained steadfast in his dedication to Yue's success.

Fan Li's legacy is that of a multifaceted figure who combined strategic brilliance, personal charisma, and unwavering loyalty to his kingdom. His relationships with key figures such as Wen Zhong, Xi Shi, and A Qing, along with his pivotal role in Yue's victory over Wu, have solidified his place as a significant historical figure in Chinese history.</data>
